# Agent Rules for Project Requirements

- When starting a requirement request, first scan all overview markdown files describing business rules, tech stack, screens, APIs, models, and source code structure in the project_root/rule/ directory.

- Analyze the requirement and update existing files or create new ones in the appropriate folders. Content updates should be comprehensive yet concise to allow for quick scanning and understanding of project structure without consuming excessive tokens.

- Create a task_list_[task].md file where [task] is a number or keyword summarizing the content of the work needed.

- Write tasks in markdown format with unchecked boxes [] for pending items and checked boxes [x] for completed implementation tasks.

- When coding, add thorough comments explaining your implementation based on requirements. Avoid deleting or modifying well-functioning code in ways that could degrade existing functionality.

- The Git repository is located in the project root folder. When asked to push files to Git, execute Git commands from the project root directory. Always use 'git add .' when committing changes.

- Before running npm install for any package, check if the package is already listed in package.json to avoid duplicate installations. Only create an installation command for packages not already present.

# Project Structure
project_root/rule/   
├── business_requirement/       # Business logic, user stories, feature specs  
├── tech_stack_requirement/     # Frameworks, libraries, tools, versions  
├── source_code_structure/      # Codebase architecture, module/class definitions  
├── screens/                    # UI/UX specs, screen components, interactions  
├── apis/                       # API endpoints, request/response formats  
├── docs/                       # (Optional) Additional technical documentation  
└── tests/                      # (Optional) Test cases, coverage reports